summary:
Let $k=\mathbb F_q$ with a fixed algebraic closure $\bar k$. Let $E,E'/k$ be elliptic curves with faithful origin-preserving actions of a nontrivial finite group $Λ$, and let $\varphi:E\to E'$ be a separable $Λ$-equivariant $k$-isogeny. Assume that the prescribed action on $E$ is stable under $q$-power Frobenius conjugation, and let $f$ be the induced map between the quotient lines. We prove that this family has no accidental permutations: for every $k_r=\mathbb F_{q^r}$, the map $f$ permutes $\mathbf P^1(k_r)$ if and only if it is exceptional over $k_r$. More precisely, writing $\mathcal N=\ker\varphi(\bar k)$, these equivalent properties are controlled entirely by the induced Frobenius and $Λ$-actions on $\mathcal N$: they hold if and only if \[
  \mathcal N\cap\ker(\mathcal F_E^r-γ_E)=\{\mathrm O_E\}
  \qquad\text{for all }γ\inΛ. \] Here $\mathrm O_E$ is the origin of $E$, $\mathcal F_E$ is $q$-power Frobenius, and $γ_E$ denotes the action of $γ$ on $E$. A weighted Frobenius-sector identity makes the criterion exact even over ramified quotient fibres. We identify the geometric and arithmetic monodromy groups as affine groups on $\mathcal N$ and prove that the permutation support is purely periodic, with explicit least positive period and natural density. For multiplication maps the criterion reduces to determinant tests on torsion, yielding explicit split and nonsplit criteria for cyclic quotient groups of orders $2$, $3$, $4$, and $6$.

